Implicit conversions from larger to smaller integer types (e.g., `long` to `int`, `int` to `char`) can lead to **data loss** or **truncation** if the value of the larger type exceeds the range of the smaller type. This can introduce subtle, hard-to-debug errors, especially in systems where data integrity is critical.
```c
long large_val = 100000L;
int small_val = large_val; // MISRA C violation: implicit conversion, potential truncation
// If int max is 32767, small_val will be truncated.
```

**Undefined behavior (UB)** occurs when the C standard imposes no requirements on a program construct. The program might crash, produce incorrect results, or appear to work unpredictably. UB makes code non-portable and unreliable.
Example of UB (out-of-bounds access):
```c
int arr[5];
int i;
for (i = 0; i <= 5; i++) { // Loop goes from 0 to 5, accessing arr[5]
    arr[i] = i;           // arr[5] is out of bounds, leading to UB
}
```
MISRA C addresses UB by restricting dangerous constructs (e.g., limiting pointer arithmetic to within array bounds) and promoting defensive programming to eliminate common sources of UB.
